TOPICS = {
"refs": """\
Naming a model

  unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF:UD-Q6_K_XL   exact repo and quant
  unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF:Q6           fuzzy quant, resolves to UD-Q6_K
  unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF              no quant given, defaults to Q4_K_M
  qwen3.8-27b                           a model you already pulled
  qwen3-coder                           not local, so Hugging Face is searched

Quant matching prefers an exact hit, then the shortest prefix, and breaks ties
toward unsloth UD- dynamic quants because they are better at the same size.

A partial ref resolves to the HIGHEST-QUALITY match, so `:Q8` gives you
UD-Q8_K_XL rather than Q8_0. Name the full quant when that matters.

Once pulled, a model gets a short name like `qwen3.8-27b:ud-q6_k_xl`. You can
use just `qwen3.8-27b` as long as it is unambiguous; if two quants of the same
model are downloaded, llcpp will say so and ask you to be specific.
""",

"quants": """\
Choosing a quant

Quantisation trades weight precision for size, and size is what determines
speed here — decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so a model half the size runs
close to twice as fast.

  Q8_0 / Q8_K_XL   ~8 bits.  Indistinguishable from full precision.
  Q6_K / Q6_K_XL   ~6 bits.  The usual sweet spot on a large-memory machine.
  Q5_K_M           ~5 bits.  Mild, rarely noticeable loss.
  Q4_K_M           ~4 bits.  The default. Good quality, half the size of Q8.
  IQ4_XS           ~4 bits.  Smaller than Q4_K_M at similar quality.
  Q3_K / IQ3       ~3 bits.  Visible degradation. Use only if it will not fit.
  Q2_K / IQ2 / IQ1 2 bits or less. Badly damaged. A last resort.
  BF16 / F16       16 bits. Twice the size of Q8 for no measurable gain.
                   `search` deliberately never recommends these.

The UD- prefix marks Unsloth Dynamic quants, which vary precision per tensor
and beat a plain quant of the same size. Prefer them when offered.

Suffixes _S, _M, _L and _XL are small/medium/large variants within a family:
larger keeps more precision in the sensitive tensors and costs a little size.
""",

"mtp": """\
MTP — multi-token prediction

Some repos ship an MTP head next to the weights, usually in an MTP/ folder.
It predicts the next token or two ahead of the main model, so llama-server can
draft several tokens and verify them in one pass. When drafts are accepted
often, decoding gets dramatically faster.

llcpp downloads that head automatically and starts the server with:

  -md <mtp.gguf> --spec-type draft-mtp --spec-draft-n-max 3 --spec-draft-n-min 1

Measured on an M5 Max with Qwen3.8-27B UD-Q6_K_XL: 55-68% of drafts accepted,
about 18 tok/s without it against 29-38 tok/s with it.

`list` marks models that have one in the FEATURES column, and `search` gives
them a ranking bonus. Skip it with `pull --no-mtp` if you want the disk back.

Reports elsewhere that MTP is a net loss come from multi-GPU NVIDIA setups,
where the recurrent-state checkpoint has to cross PCIe on every draft. On
unified memory that copy is nearly free, so the trade works out very
differently. ollama cannot do this at all.
""",

"hardware": """\
How fit and speed are estimated

  chip        machdep.cpu.brand_string
  memory      hw.memsize
  GPU budget  iogpu.wired_limit_mb, or 75% of RAM when that is unset,
              which is the macOS default cap for GPU-addressable memory

The weight budget subtracts roughly 15% plus 3 GB from that, leaving room for
the KV cache and compute buffers. Override it with `search --vram N`.

Speed estimate:

  tok/s  ~=  bandwidth / bytes-read-per-token  *  efficiency

with efficiency 0.70 for dense models and 0.55 for MoE, which pays extra for
routing. Bandwidth comes from a per-chip table; only the M5 Max figure is
vendor-confirmed, so set LLCPP_BANDWIDTH=<GB/s> if yours looks wrong.

MoE models are detected from names like 30B-A3B and estimated on their ACTIVE
parameters, which is why a 36 GB MoE can be far faster than a 25 GB dense one.

These are estimates for ranking, not benchmarks. They ignore context length —
at long prompts the bottleneck shifts from streaming weights to KV traffic and
the real number will be lower. MTP roughly doubles what is shown.
""",

"storage": """\
Where things live

